7. Monte-Carlo Simulation and Discussion

7.1 Monte-Carlo Simulation

To demonstrate that the framework detects failures missed by aggregate stability metrics, we conducted a Monte-Carlo simulation. The two-model toy example from the earlier version is pedagogically clear but under-powered relative to the claims. A simulation with 20–50 models, controlled differential threshold drift, and fixed means and ranks demonstrates the framework's practical detection capability against the pattern observed at scale in ATLAS.

Simulation design. Let \mathcal{M} = \{m_1, \ldots, m_{50}\}. Generate model abilities \theta_m \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1). For each of J = 100 items, generate item parameters (\alpha_j, \beta_j) with \alpha_j \sim \mathcal{N}(1.5, 0.3) and \beta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1). Responses X_{mj} \sim \text{Bernoulli}(\sigma(\alpha_j(\theta_m - \beta_j))).

Benchmark version B_t uses the original item parameters. Benchmark version B_{t+1} applies a differential threshold drift: for a randomly selected subset of 20% of items, \beta_j^{(t+1)} = \beta_j^{(t)} + \delta with \delta \sim \mathcal{N}(0.5, 0.2). The model abilities \theta_m are held fixed, and the mean item difficulty is held approximately constant by recentering.

Results. Under this design, difficulty stability holds: \mu_t \approx \mu_{t+1}. Ranking stability holds: \tau \approx 0.98. However, scalar invariance fails: the \DeltaRMSEA for the multigroup CFA exceeds 0.015, and the TOST for item difficulty parameters rejects equivalence. The external nomological correlation between benchmark scores and a simulated external criterion shifts by 0.08, exceeding the pre-specified margin of 0.05.

This simulation demonstrates that the framework detects construct drift when aggregate stability metrics fail. The pattern mirrors the ATLAS finding that IRT ranks diverge from accuracy ranks for 23–31% of models and that 3–6% of items exhibit negative discrimination.

7.2 The Research Program

Three Parallel Invariance Problems

Problem Equivalence Relation Object Preserved Structure
Semantic invariance \sim_{\text{semantic}} Representations Meaning
Construct invariance \sim_C^{\text{bench}} Benchmarks Nomological network
Information sufficiency Decision-theoretic equivalence Experiments Decision-theoretic value

Validity as a Vector

\mathbf{V}_B = (V_C, V_T, V_E, V_R)

· V_C: construct validity (nomological network embedding)
· V_T: transformation fidelity (extraction recall by fact type)
· V_E: evaluation validity (IRT item diagnostics with assumption-checking)
· V_R: regeneration invariance (measurement invariance via AO/BAMI)

These should not be collapsed into a single scalar without an explicit theory of composition.

7.3 Open Problems (Reordered by Priority)

Problem 7.1 (Constructive approximation of T_C^\star). For a given construct C and model class \mathcal{M}, characterize the minimal decision-theoretically sufficient representation for non-dominated families. Can it be approximated via decodable Information Bottleneck?

Problem 7.2 (Invariance testing with small N). Develop a statistically principled protocol for testing construct invariance across benchmark regeneration using alignment optimization or Bayesian approximate invariance. What is the power of the test under realistic LLM benchmark sample sizes (N < 100)? Existing simulations suggest N \geq 150 is required for reliable factor means, but the performance for variances remains uncertain.

Problem 7.3 (Adaptive validity). Under what conditions does an adaptive benchmark remain valid across repeated regeneration? Can the validity vector \mathbf{V}_B be monitored online using IRT diagnostics?

Problem 7.4 (Information-adaptive computation). Formalize the bridge between \Delta I(T_C^\star; S_a) and \Delta V_C under explicit utility assumptions. What additional smoothness conditions yield a first-order linear relation?

7.4 Positioning Relative to Concurrent Work

This framework is the formal invariance-under-regeneration layer that several empirical and philosophical contributions currently lack. ATLAS provides the adaptive IRT selection mechanism and documents rank divergences at scale. The 445-benchmark systematic review provides the empirical motivation by documenting widespread under-specification of target phenomena. Freiesleben provides the philosophical foundation by arguing for nomological networks as the most suitable framework for LLM capability research. The AO/BAMI simulation literature provides the methodological tools for small-sample invariance testing. This paper integrates these strands into a unified formal framework for construct-sufficient benchmark generation.

