A.4 Bahadur's Theorem: Existence of Minimal Sufficient Statistics

Theorem A.1 (Bahadur, 1957). Let \mathcal{P} = \{P_\theta : \theta \in \Theta\} be a family of probability measures on (\mathbb{R}^k, \mathcal{B}^k) dominated by a \sigma-finite measure \mu. Then there exists a minimal sufficient statistic T: \mathbb{R}^k \to \mathbb{R}^m for \mathcal{P}.

Proof sketch. Define the equivalence relation x \sim x' iff L_x(\theta) = c(x, x') L_{x'}(\theta) for all \theta \in \Theta, where L_x(\theta) = dP_\theta/d\mu(x) and c(x, x') > 0. Let T(x) be the equivalence class of x under this relation. By the factorization theorem for dominated families, T is sufficient. For minimality, let S be any sufficient statistic. By factorization, there exist g'_\theta and h' such that dP_\theta/d\mu(x) = g'_\theta(S(x)) \cdot h'(x). Since T(x) encodes the likelihood function up to proportionality, and the likelihood function is determined by S(x), there exists a measurable function \phi such that T(x) = \phi(S(x)) almost everywhere \mathcal{P}. Thus T is a function of every sufficient statistic, establishing minimal sufficiency. Minimal sufficient statistics exist under weak assumptions: \mathcal{P} contains distributions on \mathcal{R}^k dominated by a \sigma-finite measure. \square

Remark A.4 (Non-dominated families). The domination assumption is essential. For non-dominated families, minimal sufficient statistics may fail to exist. In such cases, the construct-sufficient representation T_C^\star must be treated as a constructive surrogate approximated via Information Bottleneck methods.

---

A.5 Uniqueness of Minimal Sufficient Statistics

Proposition A.4. If T and S are both minimal sufficient statistics for \mathcal{P}, then there exists a one-to-one measurable function \psi such that T = \psi(S) almost everywhere \mathcal{P}.

Proof. Since T is minimal sufficient, for any sufficient statistic S, there exists \psi with T = \psi(S) a.e. \mathcal{P}. Since S is also minimal sufficient, there exists \phi with S = \phi(T) a.e. \mathcal{P}. Composing, T = \psi(\phi(T)) and S = \phi(\psi(S)) a.e. \mathcal{P}. Thus \psi \circ \phi = \text{id} and \phi \circ \psi = \text{id}, establishing that \psi is a bijection. \square

---

A.6 Factorization Theorem for AMI

Proposition A.5. Suppose T_C^\star is construct-sufficient for \mathcal{P}. If:

1. D_t \sim_C^{\text{data}} D_{t+1},
2. G_1 is C-faithful: T_C^\star(D) = T_C^\star(D') \implies T_C^\star(G_1(D)) = T_C^\star(G_1(D')),
3. G_2 is C-equivariant: G_2 maps C-equivalent fact sets to C-equivalent benchmark structures,
4. E is C-valid: the evaluator orders benchmarks consistently with the nomological network of C,

then regeneration preserves C.

Proof. The proof proceeds by chasing the commutative diagram:

\begin{array}{ccc}
\mathcal{D} & \xrightarrow{G_1} & \mathcal{F} & \xrightarrow{G_2} & \mathcal{B} \\
\downarrow T_C^\star & & & & \downarrow \pi_C \\
\mathcal{T} & \xrightarrow{G_1'} & \mathcal{F}_C & \xrightarrow{G_2'} & \mathcal{B}_C
\end{array}

Step 1. By condition (1), T_C^\star(D_t) = T_C^\star(D_{t+1}) a.e. \mathcal{P}.

Step 2. By condition (2), T_C^\star(G_1(D_t)) = T_C^\star(G_1(D_{t+1})) a.e. \mathcal{P}.

Step 3. By condition (3), G_2 maps C-equivalent fact sets to C-equivalent benchmarks: G_2(G_1(D_t)) \sim_C^{\text{bench}} G_2(G_1(D_{t+1})).

Step 4. By condition (4), for all m, m' \in \mathcal{M}, S_{B_t}(m) \geq S_{B_t}(m') \iff S_{B_{t+1}}(m) \geq S_{B_{t+1}}(m'), with both orderings aligned with C. \square

