Appendix C: IRT Diagnostics and Assumption Checking

C.1 IRT Model Specification

For dichotomous items, the 2PL model is:

P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \theta_i, \alpha_j, \beta_j) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-\alpha_j(\theta_i - \beta_j))}

The 3PL model adds a guessing parameter c_j:

P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \theta_i, \alpha_j, \beta_j, c_j) = c_j + \frac{1 - c_j}{1 + \exp(-\alpha_j(\theta_i - \beta_j))}

C.2 Assumption Violations in LLM Data

Traditional 2PL IRT assumptions fail on LLM data. Many items lie at a_j \leq 0, contradicting the monotone-with-ability premise, and even when a_j > 0, empirical points do not align monotonically with ability. AI benchmark data often departs from the data regime of human testing: benchmarks typically involve fewer evaluated models, far more items, and capability distributions that may be skewed, clustered, or multimodal. Classical estimators can become infeasible in large benchmark settings, whereas scalable estimators can produce unreliable item-level and ranking inferences with small or nonnormally distributed model sets.

C.3 Assumption-Checking Procedures

Unidimensionality. Conduct EFA on the polychoric correlation matrix. Retain factors with eigenvalues > 1.

Local Independence. Compute the local dependence statistic:

LD\text{-}X^2_{jj'} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \frac{(O_{jj'k} - E_{jj'k})^2}{E_{jj'k}}

Values exceeding 10 indicate substantial local dependence.

Monotonicity. Fit a non-parametric IRT model and test whether the item response function is non-decreasing. Violations indicate that examinees with lower proficiency levels may receive higher scores.

Normality of Latent Ability Distribution. Use posterior predictive checks. Departures from normality (skewness > 2, kurtosis > 7) indicate violation.

