Appendix D: Equivalence Testing with TOST

D.1 TOST Procedure

The two one-sided tests procedure tests:

H_{0}^+: \mu_1 - \mu_2 \geq \Delta \quad \text{vs.} \quad H_{1}^+: \mu_1 - \mu_2 < \Delta

H_{0}^-: \mu_1 - \mu_2 \leq -\Delta \quad \text{vs.} \quad H_{1}^-: \mu_1 - \mu_2 > -\Delta

Equivalence is established if both null hypotheses are rejected at level \alpha. In equivalence tests, an upper and lower equivalence bound is specified based on the smallest effect size of interest. The TOST procedure can be used to statistically reject the presence of effects large enough to be considered worthwhile.

D.2 Margin Derivation

The margin \Delta is derived from the smallest effect size of interest (SESOI) in the nomological network. Pre-specify equivalence bounds before data collection.

Procedure.

1. Identify external indicators X_1, \ldots, X_K \in \mathcal{X} of construct C.
2. Estimate \rho_k = \text{Corr}(S, X_k) for each indicator.
3. Define SESOI \delta_k for each indicator.
4. Set \Delta = \min_k \delta_k.

D.3 Power Analysis

For a two-sample t-test with equal variances, the power of TOST is:

\text{Power} \approx \Phi\left(\frac{\Delta \sqrt{N/2} - z_{1-\alpha}}{\sigma}\right) - \Phi\left(\frac{-\Delta \sqrt{N/2} - z_{1-\alpha}}{\sigma}\right)

The statistical power for interval hypotheses depends on the alpha level, sample size, and the smallest effect of interest. The closer the expected effect size is to the SESOI, the larger the sample size needed to reach a desired power.

